1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:08,308
By the middle of the afternoon on the second of August, 216 BC, the Roman soldiers in the center of the line can no longer lift their arms.

2
00:00:08,808 --> 00:00:10,774
There are tens of thousands of them.

3
00:00:11,124 --> 00:00:13,624
Most have not been touched by a weapon yet.

4
00:00:13,974 --> 00:00:17,867
They are packed so tightly that they cannot swing a sword and can barely turn.

5
00:00:18,367 --> 00:00:22,429
Around them, on three sides, a smaller army is closing like a hand.

6
00:00:22,929 --> 00:00:27,734
By sunset, somewhere between forty-eight thousand and seventy thousand Romans will be dead.

7
00:00:28,084 --> 00:00:31,417
It is the worst single day in the history of the Roman army.

8
00:00:31,917 --> 00:00:36,044
And the man who did it was outnumbered, far from home, and short of food.

9
00:00:36,544 --> 00:00:42,234
So how does a general with half the soldiers destroy the largest army his enemy has ever put in the field?

10
00:00:42,734 --> 00:00:44,518
He does it with one idea.

11
00:00:44,868 --> 00:00:47,393
He makes the other side's strength the weapon.

12
00:00:47,893 --> 00:00:49,924
Let's go back to how Rome got here.

13
00:00:50,424 --> 00:00:57,586
Two years earlier, a Carthaginian general named Hannibal had marched an army, and some elephants, over the Alps and into Italy.

14
00:00:58,086 --> 00:01:01,354
Rome had expected to fight him in Spain or in Africa.

15
00:01:01,704 --> 00:01:07,823
Instead, he turned up in the Roman heartland, and in his first two battles he showed what kind of general he was.

16
00:01:08,323 --> 00:01:14,651
At the river Trebia, in the winter of 218, he lured a Roman army into freezing water and ambushed it.

17
00:01:15,001 --> 00:01:24,050
At Lake Trasimene, in the summer of 217, he hid his army in the hills beside the road and caught a Roman column in a narrow strip between the mountains and the lake.

18
00:01:24,550 --> 00:01:28,157
Ancient writers put the Roman dead there at around fifteen thousand.

19
00:01:28,657 --> 00:01:30,128
Rome was in shock.

20
00:01:30,478 --> 00:01:33,251
It did something it only did in emergencies.

21
00:01:33,601 --> 00:01:38,874
It appointed a dictator, a man named Fabius Maximus, with full power for six months.

22
00:01:39,374 --> 00:01:42,499
Fabius had a plan, and the plan was not to fight.

23
00:01:42,849 --> 00:01:47,888
He shadowed Hannibal's army, cut off foragers, harassed stragglers, and refused battle.

24
00:01:48,238 --> 00:01:49,892
His idea was simple.

25
00:01:50,241 --> 00:01:53,679
Hannibal was a long way from home with no reinforcements.

26
00:01:54,179 --> 00:01:56,366
Rome had far more men than he did.

27
00:01:56,716 --> 00:02:01,143
If Rome simply refused to give him a decisive battle, time would be on its side.

28
00:02:01,643 --> 00:02:03,492
It was a sound strategy.

29
00:02:03,842 --> 00:02:05,339
The Romans hated it.

30
00:02:05,839 --> 00:02:09,797
They called him Cunctator, the Delayer, and they did not mean it kindly.

31
00:02:10,147 --> 00:02:11,931
Soldiers wanted to fight.

32
00:02:12,281 --> 00:02:14,116
Senators wanted to win.

33
00:02:14,466 --> 00:02:20,169
Hannibal was burning farms in Roman territory, and the dictator was following him around like a tour guide.

34
00:02:20,669 --> 00:02:27,075
When Fabius's six months ended, Rome voted for new consuls, the two elected generals who commanded Rome's armies.

35
00:02:27,425 --> 00:02:34,170
The voters chose a man named Gaius Terentius Varro, who wanted to fight, and a man named Lucius Aemilius Paullus, who did not.

36
00:02:34,670 --> 00:02:38,159
And then Rome did something that no Roman army had ever done.

37
00:02:38,509 --> 00:02:41,452
It decided to settle the question once and for all.

38
00:02:41,952 --> 00:02:44,243
The plan for 216 was simple.

39
00:02:44,593 --> 00:02:48,121
Raise an army so big that Hannibal could not possibly win.

40
00:02:48,621 --> 00:02:58,947
According to the Greek historian Polybius, who is our best source, Rome put eight legions in the field, each stronger than usual, together with an equal number of infantry from its Italian allies.

41
00:02:59,297 --> 00:03:03,424
That is about eighty thousand foot soldiers, plus six thousand cavalry.

42
00:03:03,774 --> 00:03:07,797
Another ancient historian, Livy, gives slightly different figures.

43
00:03:08,297 --> 00:03:12,828
Modern historians argue about them, and some think the real numbers were lower.

44
00:03:13,178 --> 00:03:18,855
Some of those men were also left behind to guard the Roman camps, so the number on the field was smaller.

45
00:03:19,205 --> 00:03:23,306
But nobody doubts that this was the largest army Rome had ever assembled.

46
00:03:23,806 --> 00:03:27,686
Hannibal had about forty thousand infantry and ten thousand cavalry.

47
00:03:28,186 --> 00:03:29,814
Notice what that means.

48
00:03:30,164 --> 00:03:32,195
He had half as many infantry.

49
00:03:32,545 --> 00:03:37,636
But he had considerably more cavalry, and that detail is the key to everything that follows.

50
00:03:38,136 --> 00:03:40,310
His army was also a strange one.

51
00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:49,084
It was made up of Libyans, Spaniards, Gauls, and Numidian horsemen from North Africa, soldiers who spoke different languages and fought in different ways.

52
00:03:49,584 --> 00:03:54,975
Many of his Libyan infantry were armed with Roman equipment, captured at Trebia and Trasimene.

53
00:03:55,325 --> 00:03:58,801
It is the ancient version of buying your opponent's merchandise.

54
00:03:59,301 --> 00:04:01,059
And Hannibal had a problem.

55
00:04:01,409 --> 00:04:02,567
It was summer.

56
00:04:02,917 --> 00:04:04,480
His army was hungry.

57
00:04:04,980 --> 00:04:11,646
He solved it by marching south into Apulia and seizing a town called Cannae, where the Romans had stored a large supply of grain.

58
00:04:11,996 --> 00:04:13,962
The Roman army had to follow.

59
00:04:14,462 --> 00:04:18,628
A Roman general could not let a Carthaginian army sit on his granary.

60
00:04:18,978 --> 00:04:23,431
The Romans arrived and camped a few miles away on the plain beside the river Aufidus.

61
00:04:23,931 --> 00:04:25,884
Both sides knew what was coming.

62
00:04:26,234 --> 00:04:28,851
The only question was who would choose the ground.

63
00:04:29,351 --> 00:04:35,679
There is a detail about Roman command that matters here, and it is one of the strangest facts in military history.

64
00:04:36,029 --> 00:04:39,206
When two consuls led the same army, they took turns.

65
00:04:39,556 --> 00:04:41,287
One commanded one day.

66
00:04:41,637 --> 00:04:43,551
The other commanded the next.

67
00:04:44,051 --> 00:04:45,522
Paullus was cautious.

68
00:04:45,872 --> 00:04:47,474
He did not like the plain.

69
00:04:47,824 --> 00:04:52,836
It was flat, open ground, and he knew that was where Hannibal's cavalry would be at its best.

70
00:04:53,186 --> 00:04:54,566
He wanted to wait.

71
00:04:55,066 --> 00:04:56,629
Varro was aggressive.

72
00:04:56,979 --> 00:05:00,781
He wanted to fight, and he believed the huge Roman army would win.

73
00:05:01,281 --> 00:05:05,421
Livy, who wrote two hundred years later, paints Varro as a reckless fool.

74
00:05:05,771 --> 00:05:08,648
Modern historians are cautious about that picture.

75
00:05:08,998 --> 00:05:18,100
Livy was writing for a Roman audience that needed someone to blame for the disaster, and a consul who was a man of the people, not an aristocrat, made a convenient target.

76
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:22,154
Whatever the truth, the arithmetic of command did the rest.

77
00:05:22,504 --> 00:05:25,915
On the day it was Varro's turn, he led the army out of camp.

78
00:05:26,265 --> 00:05:29,625
Paullus, Polybius says, disagreed, but he followed.

79
00:05:30,125 --> 00:05:32,221
The date was the second of August.

80
00:05:32,571 --> 00:05:34,146
And Hannibal was ready.

81
00:05:34,646 --> 00:05:37,107
Hannibal had chosen his spot with care.

82
00:05:37,607 --> 00:05:40,354
The two armies met on a wide, flat plain.

83
00:05:40,704 --> 00:05:45,665
The river Aufidus ran along one side, and the Romans formed up with the river on their right.

84
00:05:46,015 --> 00:05:51,978
That meant the Roman right flank was protected by water, and it also meant that on that side they could not move away.

85
00:05:52,478 --> 00:05:54,066
There was also the wind.

86
00:05:54,416 --> 00:05:59,208
Polybius says that a hot wind blew across the plain that morning, raising clouds of dust.

87
00:05:59,558 --> 00:06:03,620
Hannibal had arranged his army so that the dust blew into Roman faces.

88
00:06:04,120 --> 00:06:08,013
Now look at the Roman deployment, because that is where the trap is hidden.

89
00:06:08,513 --> 00:06:11,807
The Romans knew they had more men, and they knew how to use them.

90
00:06:12,157 --> 00:06:20,334
In a normal Roman battle line, the infantry formed in three rows with gaps between the small units, so that each unit could move and fight on its own.

91
00:06:20,834 --> 00:06:22,305
Varro changed that.

92
00:06:22,655 --> 00:06:31,209
He packed the units closer together, and he made the formation much deeper than usual, with the aim of punching through the center of the Carthaginian line by sheer weight.

93
00:06:31,709 --> 00:06:35,850
It was a sensible plan, if you believe that Hannibal's center was weak.

94
00:06:36,200 --> 00:06:37,515
And it looked weak.

95
00:06:37,865 --> 00:06:39,258
That was the point.

96
00:06:39,758 --> 00:06:43,768
Hannibal placed his army in a shape that makes no sense at first glance.

97
00:06:44,268 --> 00:06:48,708
In the center he put his Gauls and Spaniards, the least reliable troops he had.

98
00:06:49,058 --> 00:06:51,323
But he did not put them in a straight line.

99
00:06:51,673 --> 00:06:56,178
He pushed them forward, so that the middle bulged toward the Romans like a crescent.

100
00:06:56,678 --> 00:07:03,722
On either side of this bulge, set back and out of the main line, he placed his best infantry, the Libyans, in two heavy blocks.

101
00:07:04,072 --> 00:07:06,403
On the wings, he put his cavalry.

102
00:07:06,903 --> 00:07:12,541
On the river side, facing the Roman citizen cavalry, were the heavy horsemen of Spain and Gaul.

103
00:07:12,891 --> 00:07:15,534
On the other wing were the light Numidian horse.

104
00:07:16,034 --> 00:07:18,859
Think about what the Romans see from the other side.

105
00:07:19,209 --> 00:07:21,826
A bulging center of unreliable troops.

106
00:07:22,176 --> 00:07:26,720
Exactly the kind of target that a general who wants to break the line is looking for.

107
00:07:27,220 --> 00:07:31,035
Most accounts say the plan for that center was to give ground, slowly.

108
00:07:31,535 --> 00:07:35,363
The idea was that the Gauls and Spaniards would fall back step by step.

109
00:07:35,713 --> 00:07:39,944
As they did, the crescent would straighten, then flatten, then curve inward.

110
00:07:40,294 --> 00:07:48,224
The Romans, following the retreating center, would be drawn into a pocket that had the Libyans on both sides of it and the cavalry free to close the back.

111
00:07:48,724 --> 00:07:51,510
We cannot know how much of this Hannibal controlled.

112
00:07:51,860 --> 00:07:58,552
His soldiers were under real pressure, and a center that collapsed too quickly would have let the Romans break through before the trap closed.

113
00:07:58,902 --> 00:08:01,845
The plan only worked if the middle bent without breaking.

114
00:08:02,345 --> 00:08:05,118
Ask yourself what is required for that to work.

115
00:08:05,468 --> 00:08:09,661
It needs troops who can retreat in order, under pressure, without breaking.

116
00:08:10,011 --> 00:08:12,276
A routed center would ruin everything.

117
00:08:12,626 --> 00:08:17,639
And Hannibal had somehow built an army of mixed nationalities that could do exactly that.

118
00:08:18,139 --> 00:08:22,865
The battle begins in the usual way, with skirmishers, javelins and slings.

119
00:08:23,215 --> 00:08:25,715
Then the cavalry on the river side charge.

120
00:08:26,215 --> 00:08:28,532
This is the first decisive moment.

121
00:08:28,882 --> 00:08:35,523
On the Roman right, a couple of thousand Roman citizen cavalry meet a much larger force of Spanish and Gallic heavy horse.

122
00:08:35,873 --> 00:08:40,586
The Romans are badly outnumbered, and the river beside them leaves them nowhere to go.

123
00:08:41,086 --> 00:08:44,758
Polybius says that many of the horsemen dismount and fight on foot.

124
00:08:45,108 --> 00:08:46,384
It does not help.

125
00:08:46,734 --> 00:08:47,996
They are destroyed.

126
00:08:48,496 --> 00:08:53,171
The Carthaginian cavalry commander, Hasdrubal, does not stop to celebrate.

127
00:08:53,521 --> 00:09:00,851
He leads his horsemen around the back of the whole Roman army to the other wing, where the Numidians are keeping the Roman allied cavalry busy.

128
00:09:01,201 --> 00:09:04,430
His heavy horse fall on them from behind, and they run.

129
00:09:04,930 --> 00:09:07,912
Meanwhile, in the center, the infantry clash.

130
00:09:08,262 --> 00:09:13,574
The Romans push forward, as planned, and the Gallic and Spanish center gives way, as planned.

131
00:09:13,924 --> 00:09:16,189
Step by step the crescent flattens.

132
00:09:16,539 --> 00:09:18,101
Then it goes concave.

133
00:09:18,451 --> 00:09:24,154
The Romans, delighted, pour forward into the gap, deeper and deeper, packing together as they go.

134
00:09:24,654 --> 00:09:27,141
Look at what is happening to their formation.

135
00:09:27,491 --> 00:09:30,981
The deeper they advance, the narrower the space on either side.

136
00:09:31,331 --> 00:09:34,390
The units on the edges are being squeezed toward the middle.

137
00:09:34,740 --> 00:09:38,256
Within a short time, the Roman army is not a line anymore.

138
00:09:38,606 --> 00:09:41,288
It is a dense crowd with its edges bent inward.

139
00:09:41,788 --> 00:09:43,871
And now Hannibal gives the signal.

140
00:09:44,371 --> 00:09:47,613
The Libyans, standing on either side, wheel inward.

141
00:09:47,963 --> 00:09:49,720
They hit the Roman flanks.

142
00:09:50,070 --> 00:09:55,279
At the same moment Hasdrubal's cavalry arrives behind the Roman army and attacks the rear.

143
00:09:55,629 --> 00:10:00,081
The Gauls and Spaniards in the center, who have been falling back, stop falling back.

144
00:10:00,581 --> 00:10:02,508
It is a double envelopment.

145
00:10:02,858 --> 00:10:05,332
The Romans are surrounded on four sides.

146
00:10:05,832 --> 00:10:08,149
Most of the Roman army cannot fight.

147
00:10:08,499 --> 00:10:11,533
Only the front rank on each side can reach an enemy.

148
00:10:11,883 --> 00:10:17,260
Everyone else is a spectator, pushed forward by the men behind and held in place by the men beside.

149
00:10:17,760 --> 00:10:23,112
The Romans in the middle, Polybius says, are packed so tight that they cannot use their weapons at all.

150
00:10:23,612 --> 00:10:25,448
What follows is not a battle.

151
00:10:25,798 --> 00:10:27,125
It is a slaughter.

152
00:10:27,625 --> 00:10:32,534
Step back and ask the question that the Romans must have asked themselves for years afterward.

153
00:10:32,884 --> 00:10:34,186
How did this happen?

154
00:10:34,686 --> 00:10:36,769
The first answer is the cavalry.

155
00:10:37,119 --> 00:10:40,061
Hannibal had far more of it, and it was far better.

156
00:10:40,411 --> 00:10:47,612
His heavy cavalry destroyed the Roman horse on one wing and then rode round to defeat the other, which freed them to attack the Roman rear.

157
00:10:48,112 --> 00:10:51,536
Without that, the Libyan hammer would have had no anvil.

158
00:10:51,886 --> 00:10:55,323
A Roman army with good cavalry would have kept the rear safe.

159
00:10:55,823 --> 00:10:57,828
The second answer is discipline.

160
00:10:58,178 --> 00:11:01,850
A retreating center is an extraordinary thing to ask of troops.

161
00:11:02,200 --> 00:11:05,819
Most armies, once they start moving backward, keep going.

162
00:11:06,169 --> 00:11:11,716
Hannibal's Gauls and Spaniards retreated under pressure, kept their formation, and then stopped when told to.

163
00:11:12,216 --> 00:11:18,284
The third answer is that the Romans were walking into the trap with open eyes, and the trap used their own strengths against them.

164
00:11:18,634 --> 00:11:20,860
They had more men, so they attacked.

165
00:11:21,360 --> 00:11:23,925
They had heavy infantry, so they packed it.

166
00:11:24,275 --> 00:11:27,204
They had a confident general, so he pushed hard.

167
00:11:27,554 --> 00:11:30,093
Every one of those choices was reasonable.

168
00:11:30,443 --> 00:11:32,110
Together they were fatal.

169
00:11:32,610 --> 00:11:38,013
Speaking of things that cost nothing, a like and a subscribe are free, and they keep this channel going.

170
00:11:38,363 --> 00:11:43,128
Rome, by comparison, was about to discover how expensive its lesson was.

171
00:11:43,628 --> 00:11:45,933
But the deepest answer is geometry.

172
00:11:46,433 --> 00:11:49,115
We talked about this in the story of Thermopylae.

173
00:11:49,465 --> 00:11:52,173
The number of men in a fight is not what counts.

174
00:11:52,523 --> 00:11:55,192
What counts is how many of them can reach each other.

175
00:11:55,542 --> 00:11:59,865
At Thermopylae, the Greeks used a narrow pass to make a large army small.

176
00:12:00,365 --> 00:12:02,721
At Cannae, Hannibal did the opposite.

177
00:12:03,071 --> 00:12:05,181
He did not shrink the Roman army.

178
00:12:05,531 --> 00:12:07,965
He let the Romans compress themselves.

179
00:12:08,465 --> 00:12:13,126
By the end, eighty thousand Romans were fighting with the hands of perhaps ten thousand.

180
00:12:13,476 --> 00:12:15,052
The rest were just weight.

181
00:12:15,552 --> 00:12:18,078
How many Romans actually died at Cannae?

182
00:12:18,578 --> 00:12:20,674
Polybius says seventy thousand.

183
00:12:21,024 --> 00:12:24,708
Livy, who had different sources, says about forty-eight thousand.

184
00:12:25,058 --> 00:12:30,579
A few thousand were captured in the battle, and several thousand more were taken in the Roman camp afterward.

185
00:12:30,929 --> 00:12:32,804
Only a few thousand escaped.

186
00:12:33,304 --> 00:12:37,926
Hannibal lost about six thousand men, perhaps a little more, most of them Gauls.

187
00:12:38,426 --> 00:12:41,394
Modern historians treat these figures with care.

188
00:12:41,744 --> 00:12:49,140
Ancient writers loved round numbers and dramatic contrasts, and it is not certain that the Roman army was quite as large as Polybius says.

189
00:12:49,490 --> 00:12:56,274
But even the lowest serious estimates mean that something like forty thousand Roman and allied soldiers were killed in a single afternoon.

190
00:12:56,774 --> 00:13:03,570
Some historians count it among the bloodiest single days in the history of Western warfare, and it was done with swords and spears.

191
00:13:03,920 --> 00:13:10,469
Among the dead were the consul Paullus, a large number of senators and men of senatorial rank, and a former consul.

192
00:13:10,969 --> 00:13:17,245
Livy describes Paullus refusing a horse to escape, sitting down on a rock instead, and being cut down where he sat.

193
00:13:17,595 --> 00:13:20,004
Varro escaped with a few dozen horsemen.

194
00:13:20,504 --> 00:13:23,056
Now we come to the strange part of the story.

195
00:13:23,556 --> 00:13:25,652
After Cannae, nothing happened.

196
00:13:26,152 --> 00:13:30,540
Hannibal's cavalry commander, Maharbal, urged him to march on Rome at once.

197
00:13:30,890 --> 00:13:35,655
According to Livy, he said that within five days Hannibal would be dining on the Capitol.

198
00:13:36,005 --> 00:13:38,283
Hannibal said he needed time to think.

199
00:13:38,783 --> 00:13:45,971
And Maharbal replied, in the most famous line of the whole war: "You know how to win a victory, Hannibal, but you do not know how to use one."

200
00:13:46,471 --> 00:13:50,208
It is a wonderful line, and historians doubt it ever happened.

201
00:13:50,558 --> 00:13:52,875
The truth is probably more practical.

202
00:13:53,225 --> 00:13:56,298
Hannibal's army had just fought a long, brutal day.

203
00:13:56,648 --> 00:13:58,470
He had no siege equipment.

204
00:13:58,820 --> 00:14:03,482
Rome was a huge fortified city with strong walls and a lot of men still under arms.

205
00:14:03,982 --> 00:14:08,487
And most historians think Hannibal never believed he could conquer Rome by force.

206
00:14:08,837 --> 00:14:14,396
His aim was to break up the alliance that held the Roman world together, and to make Rome sue for peace.

207
00:14:14,896 --> 00:14:16,654
So he tried diplomacy.

208
00:14:17,004 --> 00:14:19,582
He offered to let Rome ransom its prisoners.

209
00:14:20,082 --> 00:14:21,514
And Rome said no.

210
00:14:22,014 --> 00:14:24,592
Livy tells us the Senate refused to pay.

211
00:14:24,942 --> 00:14:27,624
It banned public mourning beyond thirty days.

212
00:14:27,974 --> 00:14:35,122
It raised new legions out of men who would never normally have served, including eight thousand slaves who were bought and armed at public expense.

213
00:14:35,622 --> 00:14:43,070
When the defeated consul Varro came home, the Senate went out to meet him and thanked him, not for winning, but for not despairing of the Republic.

214
00:14:43,570 --> 00:14:47,684
And the survivors of Cannae, the soldiers who had escaped, were punished.

215
00:14:48,034 --> 00:14:53,307
They were sent to Sicily, kept there in a kind of exile, and not allowed home until the war was over.

216
00:14:53,807 --> 00:14:57,831
This is the point where the Roman story and the Carthaginian story part.

217
00:14:58,181 --> 00:15:00,446
Hannibal had destroyed a Roman army.

218
00:15:00,796 --> 00:15:02,580
He had not destroyed Rome.

219
00:15:02,930 --> 00:15:07,539
Some of its southern allies, including the great city of Capua, went over to him.

220
00:15:08,039 --> 00:15:10,330
But most of the Roman alliance held.

221
00:15:10,680 --> 00:15:18,948
Rome could lose forty thousand men and recruit another army, because it had a system for turning allies and citizens into soldiers that Hannibal could not match.

222
00:15:19,448 --> 00:15:22,378
Hannibal stayed in Italy for another thirteen years.

223
00:15:22,728 --> 00:15:23,951
He won battles.

224
00:15:24,301 --> 00:15:25,707
He never took Rome.

225
00:15:26,057 --> 00:15:30,198
He came to within a few miles of its walls in 211, and went away again.

226
00:15:30,698 --> 00:15:33,002
So what did Cannae actually achieve?

227
00:15:33,502 --> 00:15:37,330
As a battle, it is the most perfect victory in the ancient world.

228
00:15:37,680 --> 00:15:41,091
Military academies have taught it for two thousand years.

229
00:15:41,591 --> 00:15:54,846
The German general Alfred von Schlieffen was so obsessed with the idea of a perfect encirclement that he wrote a book about it, and his famous plan for the invasion of France in 1914 is often described as an attempt to repeat Cannae on a continental scale.

230
00:15:55,346 --> 00:15:56,349
It failed.

231
00:15:56,849 --> 00:15:59,205
As a strategy, it did not work.

232
00:15:59,555 --> 00:16:02,107
Hannibal won the battle and lost the war.

233
00:16:02,607 --> 00:16:06,357
There is a lesson in that, and it is a harder one than the tactics.

234
00:16:06,707 --> 00:16:10,925
A spectacular victory does not win a war if the other side can absorb it.

235
00:16:11,275 --> 00:16:12,786
Rome could absorb it.

236
00:16:13,136 --> 00:16:16,807
Carthage, which could not replace Hannibal's veterans, could not.

237
00:16:17,307 --> 00:16:18,596
And Rome learned.

238
00:16:18,946 --> 00:16:22,800
Fabius had been right in the first place, and it took Cannae to prove it.

239
00:16:23,150 --> 00:16:31,705
After 216, Rome returned to the strategy of avoiding Hannibal in open battle while it won the war elsewhere, in Sicily, in Spain, and in Africa.

240
00:16:32,205 --> 00:16:35,681
The man who finally beat Hannibal was a Roman named Scipio.

241
00:16:36,031 --> 00:16:38,752
As a young officer, he had survived Cannae.

242
00:16:39,252 --> 00:16:51,348
In 202 BC, at a battle called Zama in North Africa, which ended the war with a peace the following year, he used the same weapon that Hannibal had used against his army, a cavalry charge that swept round to the back of the enemy.

243
00:16:51,848 --> 00:16:54,686
He had studied the man who had destroyed his friends.

244
00:16:55,186 --> 00:16:58,038
Cannae is not just a story about a clever plan.

245
00:16:58,388 --> 00:17:02,906
It is a story about what happens when a larger force mistakes numbers for strength.

246
00:17:03,406 --> 00:17:06,583
At Thermopylae, space made a small army strong.

247
00:17:06,933 --> 00:17:09,797
At Cannae, space made a big army helpless.

248
00:17:10,297 --> 00:17:15,349
The Romans did not lose because they were afraid or badly led, although they were both a little.

249
00:17:15,699 --> 00:17:21,792
They lost because they walked into a shape that had been built for them, and they walked in with every man pushing the man in front.

250
00:17:22,292 --> 00:17:27,396
Hannibal's real weapon at Cannae was not the cavalry, or the Libyans, or even the crescent.

251
00:17:27,746 --> 00:17:31,197
It was his understanding of what the other side wanted to do.

252
00:17:31,697 --> 00:17:34,548
If you take one thing from this story, take this.

253
00:17:34,898 --> 00:17:38,257
The most dangerous trap is one that looks like an opportunity.

254
00:17:38,757 --> 00:17:41,114
And count the people who can reach each other.

255
00:17:41,464 --> 00:17:43,443
Not the people standing there.
